window.L3_DATA={chapters:[
{id:'rational',title:'Rational numbers',icon:'−¾',place:'The lift and the measuring jug',story:'A lift can be above or below the ground floor. A jug can hold part of a litre. Rational numbers let us describe both signs and equal parts.',steps:[
['A fraction can name a number','A rational number can be written p/q, where p and q are integers and q is not zero. Integers are whole numbers and their negatives. For example, −3/4 is rational.','p/q, with q ≠ 0'],
['Zero and whole numbers fit too','5 = 5/1, and 0 = 0/1. The bottom cannot be zero: dividing something into zero equal groups does not give a usable answer.','5 = 5/1; 0 = 0/1'],
['Find its place on the line','Split the distance from 0 to 1 into four equal steps. Three steps right is 3/4. Three steps left is −3/4. A number farther right is greater.','−3/4 < 0 < 3/4'],
['Same location, different names','−1/2 and −2/4 land in the same place. Multiplying top and bottom by the same nonzero number keeps the share unchanged.','−1/2 = −2/4'],
['Add by walking','To find −3/4 + 1/2, first write the half as 2/4. Start at −3/4 and move two quarter-steps right. You arrive at −1/4.','−3/4 + 2/4 = −1/4'],
['Subtract by undoing a move','Taking away a negative move is the same as adding a positive move. 1/4 − (−1/2) = 1/4 + 2/4 = 3/4. Check: 3/4 + (−1/2) = 1/4.','1/4 − (−1/2) = 3/4'],
['Multiply and divide shares','Half of three quarters is three eighths: 1/2 × 3/4 = 3/8. For 3/4 ÷ 1/2, ask how many halves fit into three quarters: one and a half. Multiplying by the reciprocal gives 3/4 × 2/1 = 3/2.','3/4 ÷ 1/2 = 3/2'],
['Decimals can be rational','0.75 = 75/100 = 3/4. Repeating decimals also count: 0.333… = 1/3. A minus sign belongs to the value, whether written above, below or before the fraction.','−3/4 = 3/(−4)']
]},
{id:'linear',title:'Linear equations',icon:'2x',place:'The school fair ticket desk',story:'Two game tickets and a ₹3 badge cost ₹15. What does one ticket cost? Use a balanced equation to work backwards.',steps:[
['Name one unknown','Let x be the cost of ONE ticket. Two tickets cost x + x, written 2x. Add the badge to make the whole bill.','2x + 3 = 15'],
['Undo the addition first','Take the ₹3 badge away from both sides of the equation. This keeps the two sides equal. On the left only two ticket prices remain.','2x = 15 − 3 = 12'],
['Share into equal groups','Two equal ticket prices make ₹12. Divide both sides by 2 to find one ticket: x = 6.','x = 12 ÷ 2 = 6'],
['Put it back into the story','Two ₹6 tickets cost ₹12. Add the ₹3 badge and the total is ₹15. So our answer fits the original bill.','2 × 6 + 3 = 15 ✓'],
['Letters can appear on both sides','For 3x + 2 = x + 10, remove one x from BOTH sides. You have 2x + 2 = 10. Remove 2 from both sides to get 2x = 8. Divide by 2: x = 4.','3x + 2 = x + 10 → x = 4'],
['Open brackets carefully','2(x + 3) means TWO copies of the whole group x + 3. That is x + 3 + x + 3 = 2x + 6. So 2(x + 3) = 14 becomes 2x + 6 = 14, then x = 4.','2(x + 3) = 2x + 6'],
['Clear a fraction fairly','For x/3 + 2 = 5, subtract 2 from both sides: x/3 = 3. Multiply both sides by 3 to undo dividing by 3. x = 9. Check: 9/3 + 2 = 5.','x/3 = 3 → x = 9'],
['Why is it called linear?','The unknown has power 1. Relations such as y = 2x + 3 make straight lines on a graph. Equations help us find missing prices, distances and times.','x¹ = x']
]},
{id:'quads',title:'Understanding quadrilaterals',icon:'▱',place:'The picture-frame workshop',story:'Four pieces of wood make a frame. Which corners must match? Which sides must stay parallel? Learn to use properties, rather than guessing from a picture.',steps:[
['Start with four straight sides','A quadrilateral is a simple four-sided shape. Simple means its outline does not cross itself. Its interior angles are the angles inside its corners.','4 sides; 4 interior angles'],
['See why the angles total 360°','Join two opposite corners with an interior diagonal. The shape splits into two triangles. Each contributes 180°, giving 360° altogether.','180° + 180° = 360°'],
['Compare rectangle and square','Both have four right angles. A square also has four equal sides. Every square is a rectangle, but a long, narrow rectangle need not be a square.','Square = rectangle + all sides equal'],
['Meet the parallelogram family','A parallelogram has two pairs of parallel opposite sides. Its opposite sides and angles match. Neighbouring angles add to 180° because the sides are parallel.','65° + 115° = 180°'],
['Distinguish a rhombus','A rhombus has four equal sides. Its angles need not be 90°. A square is both a rectangle and a rhombus. Turning the drawing does not change the shape’s properties.','Equal sides alone do not force right angles'],
['Trapezium and kite','A trapezium (trapezoid) has a pair of parallel sides. Angles along each leg total 180°. A kite has two pairs of equal neighbouring sides, and the opposite angles between unequal sides match.','Use side clues before naming the shape'],
['Use diagonal clues','A parallelogram’s diagonals cut each other in half. A rectangle’s diagonals are also equal in length. A rhombus’s diagonals meet at right angles. A square has all these properties.','A diagonal joins opposite corners'],
['Solve, then check','Three angles are 85°, 95° and 110°. Add them: 290°. The missing corner is 360° − 290° = 70°. Check the four together total 360°.','85° + 95° + 110° + 70° = 360°']
]},
{id:'roots',title:'Squares and square roots',icon:'√',place:'The tiled reading corner',story:'The reading corner needs 64 square tiles. Can you arrange them into a square? A square root works backwards from the area to the side length.',steps:[
['Build the square','A square with n tiles along each side has n rows of n tiles. Multiply n by itself to count the whole square.','8² = 8 × 8 = 64'],
['Reverse that question','If the area is 64, ask which nonnegative number multiplied by itself makes 64. The answer is 8. This is the square root of 64.','√64 = 8'],
['Know what the symbol means','√64 names the principal, nonnegative square root: 8. The equation x² = 64 has TWO real answers, 8 and −8. A physical side length uses the positive one.','√64 = 8; x² = 64 → x = ±8'],
['Use pairs of factors','36 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 3. Put matching factors into pairs. Take one factor from each pair to form the square’s side: 2 × 3 = 6.','√36 = √(2² × 3²) = 6'],
['Not every root is a whole number','49 = 7² and 64 = 8². Since 50 is between 49 and 64, √50 is between 7 and 8. It is about 7.07, not exactly 7.07.','7 < √50 < 8'],
['Check with the reverse operation','If you think √81 is 9, multiply 9 × 9. You get 81, so it works. If you tried 8, 8 × 8 = 64 would be too small.','9² = 81 ✓'],
['Know the real-number limit','No real number squared gives a negative result. In this course, √(−9) has no real value. We are working with real numbers.','A real square is never negative']
]},
{id:'cuberoots',title:'Cubes and cube roots',icon:'∛',place:'The storage-box builder',story:'Build a storage cube from little unit cubes. Turn the 3D model, count a layer, then stack layers. A cube root finds the side length from the volume.',steps:[
['Build one floor','A cube with side 4 has 4 rows of 4 small cubes on its bottom layer. That makes 16 small cubes in one layer.','4 × 4 = 16 per layer'],
['Stack four floors','The cube is 4 layers tall. Multiply the 16 cubes in one layer by 4 layers: 64 unit cubes altogether.','4³ = 4 × 4 × 4 = 64'],
['Ask the question backwards','A cube root asks which number multiplied by itself THREE times makes the volume. Since 4 × 4 × 4 = 64, the cube root of 64 is 4.','∛64 = 4'],
['Group prime factors in threes','216 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 3 × 3 × 3. Take one factor from each matching triple: 2 × 3 = 6. That is the side length.','∛216 = 6'],
['Negative cubes keep their sign','(−3) × (−3) is 9. Then 9 × (−3) is −27. So ∛(−27) = −3. The drawing shows physical positive volumes; negative values are algebra examples.','(−3)³ = −27'],
['Compare square root and cube root','Square roots undo two equal factors. Cube roots undo three. √64 = 8 because 8 × 8 = 64; ∛64 = 4 because 4 × 4 × 4 = 64.','√64 = 8; ∛64 = 4'],
['Check your side length','For volume 125, try side 5. One layer has 25 cubes, and 5 layers have 125. So ∛125 = 5. Hidden cubes inside the solid count too.','5 × 5 × 5 = 125 ✓']
]},
{id:'compare',title:'Comparing quantities',icon:'%',place:'The school fair shop',story:'Compare prices, discounts, profit and tax without getting tricked by the word percent. Always ask: a percentage of WHICH amount?',steps:[
['Ratios compare like quantities','A recipe has 2 cups of juice for 3 cups of water. Its ratio is 2:3. The total has 5 equal parts, so juice is 2/5, or 40%, of the whole drink.','2/(2 + 3) = 2/5 = 40%'],
['Find a percentage','Percent means out of 100. To find 15% of ₹200, find 1% first: ₹200 ÷ 100 = ₹2. Take 15 parts: ₹2 × 15 = ₹30.','15% of ₹200 = ₹30'],
['Discount: find the saving first','A ₹200 item with 15% off saves ₹30. Subtract that saving from the marked price: ₹200 − ₹30 = ₹170 to pay.','Price after discount = ₹170'],
['Tax: use the price it applies to','If 10% tax is then charged on ₹170, tax is ₹17. Add it to the discounted price: ₹170 + ₹17 = ₹187. Here the tax is on ₹170, not ₹200.','₹170 × 10/100 = ₹17 tax'],
['Profit and loss use cost price','Buy an item for ₹80 and sell it for ₹100. Profit is ₹20. Compare profit with the COST: 20/80 × 100 = 25%. It is not 20% just because the selling price is 100.','Profit % = profit ÷ cost × 100'],
['A percentage change needs a starting point','A price rises from ₹50 to ₹60: increase ₹10. Compare with the original ₹50: 10/50 × 100 = 20%. The reverse fall is 10/60 × 100, about 16.67%.','Same ₹10 change, different percentage'],
['Revisit simple and compound growth','At 10% yearly, ₹1,000 earns ₹100 each year under simple interest. With annual compounding, year 2 earns on ₹1,100, so it adds ₹110. Two-year totals are ₹1,200 and ₹1,210.','Always identify the amount earning the percentage']
]}
],questions:[]};
(function(){const q=(topic,prompt,answer,others,steps)=>L3_DATA.questions.push({topic,prompt,answer,options:[answer,...others],steps});
q('rational','Which number is greater: −3/4 or −1/2?','−1/2',['−3/4','They are equal','Neither is rational'],['Match pieces: −1/2 = −2/4.','Compare: −2/4 is to the right of −3/4 on a number line. Numbers farther right are greater.','Check: Starting at −3/4, adding 1/4 reaches −2/4. So −1/2 is greater.']);
q('rational','Calculate −3/4 + 1/2.','−1/4',['−5/4','1/4','−1/2'],['Make the pieces match: 1/2 = 2/4.','Add the counts: −3 quarters + 2 quarters = −1 quarter.','Check: −1/4 − 2/4 = −3/4, returning the start.']);
q('rational','What is 3/4 ÷ 1/2?','3/2',['3/8','2/3','1/4'],['Ask: How many half-sized portions fit into three quarters? One half fits, with a quarter left.','The leftover quarter is half of a half-portion. So there are 1 1/2 portions, or 3/2.','Check: 3/2 × 1/2 = 3/4. Dividing by a nonzero fraction equals multiplying by its reciprocal.']);
q('rational','Which cannot be used as the denominator of a rational number?','0',['1','−2','5'],['Definition: A rational number is p/q with integer p and q, and q must not be zero.','Reason: Dividing by zero does not define a number here.','Check: Negative denominators are allowed: 3/(−2) = −3/2. Zero is the exception.']);
q('linear','Two tickets and a ₹3 badge cost ₹15. Find one ticket’s price.','₹6',['₹9','₹12','₹7.50'],['Write: 2x + 3 = 15, where x is one ticket price.','Subtract 3 from BOTH sides: 2x = 12.','Divide both sides by 2: x = 6.','Check: 2 × 6 + 3 = 15.']);
q('linear','Solve 3x + 2 = x + 10.','4',['8','6','12'],['Remove one x from both sides: 2x + 2 = 10.','Remove 2 from both sides: 2x = 8.','Divide both sides by 2: x = 4.','Check: 3 × 4 + 2 = 14, and 4 + 10 = 14. Both sides match.']);
q('linear','Solve 2(x + 3) = 14.','4',['7','5.5','11'],['The bracket is one group: two equal groups total 14, so one group equals 7.','Divide both sides by 2: x + 3 = 7.','Subtract 3 from both sides: x = 4.','Check: 2 × (4 + 3) = 2 × 7 = 14.']);
q('linear','Solve x/3 + 2 = 5.','9',['1','21','3'],['Subtract 2 from both sides: x/3 = 3.','Undo dividing by 3 by multiplying BOTH sides by 3: x = 9.','Check: 9/3 + 2 = 3 + 2 = 5.']);
q('quads','A four-sided park has angles 85°, 95°, 110° and x. Find x.','70°',['110°','80°','290°'],['Total: A simple quadrilateral’s interior angles sum to 360°.','Add the known angles: 85 + 95 + 110 = 290°.','Subtract: x = 360 − 290 = 70°.','Check: 85 + 95 + 110 + 70 = 360.']);
q('quads','Which statement is always true?','Every square is a rectangle',['Every rectangle is a square','Every kite is a square','Every rhombus has right angles'],['A rectangle needs four right angles. A square has four right angles too.','A square adds another condition: all sides have equal length.','Check: A long narrow rectangle has right angles but unequal neighbouring sides. So the reverse statement is not always true.']);
q('quads','A parallelogram has one angle of 65°. Its neighbour is…','115°',['65°','25°','295°'],['Neighbouring interior angles between the parallel sides total 180°.','Subtract the known turn: 180 − 65 = 115°.','Check: 65 + 115 = 180. The OPPOSITE angle would be 65°, not the neighbour.']);
q('quads','Which diagonal property always holds in a parallelogram?','They cut each other in half',['They are always equal in length','They always meet at 90°','They never meet'],['Each diagonal joins two opposite corners.','Their meeting point is halfway along each diagonal. This property is called bisecting each other.','Check the distinction: Equal diagonal lengths need the rectangle property; a right-angle crossing holds for a rhombus. A general parallelogram need not have either.']);
q('roots','What does √64 equal?','8',['8 and −8','32','4'],['The √ symbol asks for the nonnegative number whose square is 64.','8 × 8 = 64, so √64 = 8.','Check the wording: The equation x² = 64 has ±8, but the symbol √64 names just the nonnegative root.']);
q('roots','Which two consecutive whole numbers surround √50?','7 and 8',['5 and 6','6 and 7','8 and 9'],['Square 7: 7 × 7 = 49.','Square 8: 8 × 8 = 64.','Since 49 < 50 < 64, the nonnegative root lies between 7 and 8.','Check: √50 is about 7.07, which fits that interval.']);
q('roots','Use factor pairs to find √36.','6',['18','3','12'],['Break 36 into factors: 2 × 2 × 3 × 3.','Group matching pairs: (2 × 2)(3 × 3).','One factor from each pair gives the side: 2 × 3 = 6.','Check: 6 × 6 = 36.']);
q('roots','A square floor has area 81 m². Find the side length.','9 m',['9 m²','40.5 m','−9 m'],['A square’s area is side × side. Work backwards using √81.','9 × 9 = 81, so the positive side length is 9.','Check units: 9 m × 9 m = 81 m². A side length uses metres, not square metres.']);
q('cuberoots','What is ∛64?','4',['8','16','32'],['Look for three equal factors, not two.','4 × 4 = 16, and 16 × 4 = 64.','So ∛64 = 4. Check: 4³ = 64.']);
q('cuberoots','A cube contains 125 unit cubes. Its side is…','5 units',['25 units','3 units','125 units'],['A side of 5 gives 5 × 5 = 25 cubes in one layer.','There are 5 layers, so 25 × 5 = 125 cubes.','Check: Width × depth × height = 5 × 5 × 5 = 125.']);
q('cuberoots','What is ∛(−27) over the real numbers?','−3',['3','No real value','−9'],['Cube −3: (−3) × (−3) = 9.','Multiply once more: 9 × (−3) = −27.','Check: Cubing −3 returns −27, so its cube root is −3.']);
q('cuberoots','216 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 3 × 3 × 3. Find ∛216.','6',['12','36','9'],['Find triples: three copies of 2 and three copies of 3.','Take one factor from each triple: 2 × 3 = 6.','Check: 6 × 6 × 6 = 216.']);
q('compare','A ₹200 item has 15% off. What do you pay before tax?','₹170',['₹30','₹185','₹230'],['Find 1%: ₹200 ÷ 100 = ₹2.','15% saved: ₹2 × 15 = ₹30.','Pay: ₹200 − ₹30 = ₹170.','Check: ₹170 paid + ₹30 saved = ₹200.']);
q('compare','10% tax is charged on a discounted price of ₹170. Total payment?','₹187',['₹180','₹17','₹190'],['The tax applies to ₹170. One tenth of ₹170 is ₹17.','Add the tax to the price: ₹170 + ₹17 = ₹187.','Check: ₹187 − ₹170 = ₹17, exactly 10% of ₹170.']);
q('compare','Buy for ₹80, sell for ₹100. What is the profit percentage?','25%',['20%','80%','125%'],['Profit is the money above cost: ₹100 − ₹80 = ₹20.','Compare with COST price: 20/80 = 1/4.','Turn into percent: 1/4 × 100 = 25%.','Check: 25% of ₹80 is ₹20. Adding that profit returns the ₹100 sale price.']);
q('compare','Juice and water are mixed in a 2:3 ratio. What percentage is juice?','40%',['66.67%','60%','20%'],['Find total parts: 2 + 3 = 5.','Juice is 2 of those 5 parts, or 2/5 of the whole.','Multiply by 100: 2/5 × 100 = 40%.','Check: Water is 3/5 = 60%. Together 40% + 60% = 100%.']);
})();
